# Formatting with Prettier

`@mdsvex/prettier-plugin` formats documents with [Prettier](https://prettier.io).

::::plate[Adding the plugin](mark=".svx", tone=text)
Install it beside Prettier, and add it to the `plugins` of your Prettier config:

```bash
pnpm add --save-dev prettier @mdsvex/prettier-plugin
```

```json
{
	"plugins": ["@mdsvex/prettier-plugin"]
}
```

:::notes
Prettier picks the plugin for `.svx` and `.pfm` files from their extension.
:::

:::edges
- For another extension in `extensions`, add an override that sets `"parser": "mdsvex"` for it.
:::
::::

::::plate[Svelte, as prettier-plugin-svelte formats it](mark="{#if}", tone=logic)
The plugin formats the Svelte in a document as [prettier-plugin-svelte](https://github.com/sveltejs/prettier-plugin-svelte) formats a component.

:::notes
A tag that does not fit on a line puts its attributes one to a line, the children of elements, components and blocks such as `{#if}` are indented, markdown included, and scripts move to the top and styles to the end as `svelteSortOrder` says.
:::
::::

::::plate[Markdown, as Prettier formats it](mark="-", tone=list)
The markdown is formatted as Prettier formats markdown: `-` bullets, ordered lists counted from their start, padded and aligned tables, a blank line between blocks, list items lined up with their content and `> ` on every line of a quote.

:::notes
`proseWrap` works as it does for markdown, `always` fills the line and `never` puts a paragraph on one line.
:::

:::edges
- Emphasis stays as PFM writes it, `_` for emphasis and `*` for strong.
:::
::::

::::plate[The code in a document](mark="{}", tone=expr)
The code in a document is formatted with your Prettier options:

| part | formatted |
| --- | --- |
| frontmatter | as yaml, unless the project parses it with [`frontmatter.parse`](/docs/guides/frontmatter#custom-frontmatter-parsers) |
| imports, `<script>`, `<style>` | as code, TypeScript with `lang="ts"`, scss or less with `lang` |
| `{expression}`, `{@html}`, `{@render}`, `{@const}` | on one line |
| the heads of `{#if}`, `{#each}`, `{#await}`, `{#key}`, `{#snippet}` | on one line |
| attribute expressions | on one line, the tag keeps its line breaks and the order of its attributes |
| code fences | kept as written, see `mdsvexFormatFences` below |

:::edges
- A part that does not parse is kept as written.
:::
::::

## Plugin options

::::plate[`mdsvexFormatFences`](mark="```", tone=fence)
`false` by default. With it on, the plugin formats a fence whose info string is only a language Prettier knows:

```json
{
	"plugins": ["@mdsvex/prettier-plugin"],
	"mdsvexFormatFences": true
}
```

:::edges
- A fence with meta, such as a line range, or with an annotation is still kept, as its line numbers count the lines of the code.
:::
::::

::::plate[`mdsvexFrontmatter`](mark="---", tone=text)
`"auto"` by default. `"auto"` formats frontmatter as yaml unless the manifest the Vite plugin writes says the project parses it itself, `"yaml"` always formats it and `"keep"` never does:

```json
{
	"plugins": ["@mdsvex/prettier-plugin"],
	"mdsvexFrontmatter": "keep"
}
```
::::

## Leaving parts alone

::::plate[`prettier-ignore`](mark="<!--", tone=other)
`<!-- prettier-ignore -->` keeps the block after it, or the next node when it sits in a paragraph:

```svx
<!-- prettier-ignore -->
{items.map((item)=>item.name)}
```
::::

::::plate[`prettier-ignore-start` and `-end`](mark="start", tone=other)
Everything between `<!-- prettier-ignore-start -->` and `<!-- prettier-ignore-end -->` at the top of a document is kept:

```svx
<!-- prettier-ignore-start -->
| kept | as  |
| ---- | --- |
| it   | is  |
<!-- prettier-ignore-end -->
```
::::

`<!-- @format -->` at the top, or after the frontmatter, works with `--require-pragma` and `--insert-pragma`.
